Now we need to produce a plan that achieves all these.

We must respect actions and their preconditions.

We have many actions defined.

We need to prepare origin_availability_alpha, prepare shift_candidate_beta, link task profile, link vehicle, allocate vehicle, etc.

Also need to load containers, assemble transport leg, etc.

Goal also requires loading_container_leg_linked loading_container_beta transport_leg_alpha and loading_container_loaded loading_container_beta. So we need to load loading_container_beta onto transport_leg_alpha.

Goal also requires transport_leg_assembled, transport_leg_origin_linked, transport_leg_destination_linked, transport_leg_loading_authorized.

Goal also requires origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready, destination_node_occupied and destination_node_ready (destination already has both). So we need to make origin node occupied and ready.

Goal also requires origin_availability_confirmed and origin_availability_locked (both). Destination already has those.

Goal also requires dispatch_allocated for origin_availability_alpha, destination_availability_alpha, shift_candidate_beta.

Goal also requires dispatch_ready for those three.

Goal also requires dispatched for those three.

Goal also requires candidate_dispatch_slot_linked for origin_availability_alpha, destination_availability_alpha, shift_candidate_beta (all to dispatch_slot_alpha). Note that dispatch_slot_alpha can only be linked to one candidate? Actually the action allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate has precondition dispatch_slot_available and effect not dispatch_slot_available (i.e., consumes it). So only one candidate can have dispatch_slot_allocated at a time. However goal wants three different candidates each linked to same dispatch_slot_alpha. That seems impossible because dispatch_slot_available is a resource that can be allocated to only one candidate at a time. But maybe the predicate candidate_dispatch_slot_linked does not consume availability? Actually allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate effect: (dispatch_allocated ?candidate) and (candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot) and (not (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot)). So after allocation, dispatch_slot_available becomes false. So you cannot allocate the same slot to multiple candidates simultaneously. However the goal may be achievable if we allocate and then deallocate? But there is no action to release dispatch slot. There is no action that makes dispatch_slot_available again. The only way to make dispatch_slot_available true is via the effects of execute_*_dispatch actions: they have effect (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). Indeed, execute_origin_dispatch, execute_destination_dispatch, execute_schedule_dispatch each have effect (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot) and (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit) and (dispatched ?candidate). So after executing a dispatch, the dispatch slot becomes available again. So we could allocate, then dispatch, which releases the slot, then allocate again for another candidate, dispatch, etc. So we can achieve dispatch_allocated and dispatched for each candidate sequentially, and after each dispatch the slot becomes available again for next allocation.

Thus we need to perform for each candidate: allocate dispatch slot, then execute dispatch (which will set dispatched true, dispatch_slot_available true, vehicle_available true). However note that execute_*_dispatch also requires dispatch_allocated ?candidate (precondition) and candidate_vehicle_linked ?candidate ?vehicle_unit and candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot and not dispatched ?candidate. So we need to have those links before executing dispatch.

After dispatch, we get dispatched true, vehicle_available true, dispatch_slot_available true. Also note that dispatch_allocated remains true? The effect does not retract dispatch_allocated. So after dispatch, dispatch_allocated stays true. That's fine because goal wants dispatch_allocated true.

Thus we can allocate, then execute dispatch, and we will have dispatch_allocated true, dispatched true, dispatch_slot_available true (again). So we can repeat.

Now we need to also have candidate_dispatch_slot_linked true after dispatch (it remains true because effect does not retract it). So after dispatch we still have the link.

Thus we can allocate, dispatch, allocate again for next candidate, dispatch, etc.

Now we need to also ensure vehicle_available for alpha and beta after dispatch? Actually after executing dispatch for a candidate, the effect includes (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit). So whichever vehicle unit is linked to that candidate becomes available. So we need to ensure that for origin_availability_alpha, its vehicle_unit_alpha becomes available after its dispatch. Similarly for destination_availability_alpha, vehicle_unit_beta becomes available after its dispatch. For shift_candidate_beta, vehicle_unit_gamma becomes available after its dispatch. However goal also requires vehicle_available vehicle_unit_alpha and vehicle_available vehicle_unit_beta (true after their dispatches). It does NOT require vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma; indeed after dispatch of shift_candidate_beta, gamma would become available, but goal does not require it; it's okay if it becomes available (not prohibited). However we must check if any goal condition expects vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma? No. So it's fine if gamma becomes available; but we might want to keep it allocated? Actually after dispatch, vehicle_available gamma becomes true, which may conflict with goal? Goal does not require gamma not available, so it's okay.

Now we need to achieve many other preconditions: candidate_prepared, candidate_task_profile_linked, candidate_vehicle_linked, candidate_operator_linked, candidate_skill_certificate_linked, candidate_compliance_certificate_linked, candidate_staged_for_loading, candidate_loading_verified, candidate_clearance_established, candidate_finalized, etc.

